1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:07,040
let's now have a look at multi-threading 
models there are two ways in which you can

2
00:00:09,360 --> 00:00:16,240
get your application or your process to run in a 
multi-threaded fashion you can go for user threads  

3
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:23,760
so you can manage threads completely at the user 
level there are libraries for that of course  

4
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:30,240
you don't have to do everything yourself but if 
you think about it for a moment what I'm trying to  

5
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:38,880
say here is that in principle you could write 
your code so that instead of just running the  

6
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:45,040
next instruction of a certain function you 
have some meta code meta instructions that  

7
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:52,800
periodically check let's say which function to run 
and then select one appropriately okay so you can  

8
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:59,120
manage threads at the user level you don't have 
to do everything yourself you can do it at the

9
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:07,120
at the user level to make a very 
very simple example if you have a process  

10
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:13,280
and this process should run the 
spell checker but also

11
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:22,640
run something else for example check if there 
are software updates from the internet  

12
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:29,680
you could write a function that checks for updates 
a function that runs the spell 

13
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:36,080
checker okay but these functions could be written 
in a way so that periodically they get back to  

14
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:43,120
another function and this function is the one that 
decides which one of the two to run so you can  

15
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:47,760
emulate threads at the user level 
by writing code that makes sure that  

16
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:52,160
let's say half of the time you do something 
and also the time you're doing something else  

17
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:57,520
you can do all these things at the user level 
on the other end you can do this at the  

18
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:04,400
kernel level letting the threads 
operate inside the kernel and with this is  

19
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:11,280
what you usually do with modern operating system 
and modern hardware so there are different  

20
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:21,200
threading models there is the many to one one to one 
and many too many and I understand at this point  

21
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:25,360
some of them might sound a bit strange but 
hopefully during this lecture and during  

22
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:31,120
also the following one on scheduling it will get a 
bit clearer why you want to do something like this  

23
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:40,880
if you go to many-to-one threads what you 
do is that you define several threads at the user  

24
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:46,880
level but you define a single thread at the kernel 
level so from the operating system perspective and  

25
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:53,280
from the hardware perspective you are running a 
process that is running a single thread  

26
00:02:53,280 --> 00:03:02,400
but at the user level using a supporting 
library you can periodically change let's say  

27
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:07,760
which function you run which portion of code 
so that you can achieve for example balance  

28
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:13,600
between the different functions what is tricky here 
is that since the operating system

29
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:20,640
sees everything as a 
single thread as soon as one of your user threads  

30
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:27,600
request something through a system call the 
entire process will go into waiting mode  

31
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:34,480
so in this case it's useless to have 
many user level threads if one of them  

32
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:39,520
tries to interact with the operating system through 
a system call or generates an interrupt because  

33
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:45,760
all of them will be put into waiting because for 
the operating system everything is a single thread  

34
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:53,280
the multithreaded execution is being 
managed at the user level the other alternative  

35
00:03:53,280 --> 00:04:01,520
is to go one to one for each user level thread you 
define a kernel thread in this case if your  

36
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,200
thread that checks for updates from the internet 
requires interaction with the operating system  

37
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:13,280
through a system call because it needs to check 
something that needs to go through the network  

38
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:19,280
card that thread can be put into waiting but 
the other thread that runs the spell checker and  

39
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:23,280
it doesn't really need input/output with the 
hardware because it just needs to check what  

40
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:28,800
is in memory and whether what your writing is 
correct or not can keep running of course this  

41
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:33,520
takes more resources for the operating system 
because you have to create separate threads and  

42
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:37,840
the operating system needs to be aware that these 
are different threads that belong to the same process  

43
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:42,960
and as you might imagine it's also 
less efficient because here you can  

44
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:47,600
have context switches between the different 
threads at the operating system level

45
00:04:50,560 --> 00:04:55,680
the more general one or let's say the 
one in in between is the many-to-many

46
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:03,920
model so you could say well there are a certain 
number of user-level threads and these are mapped  

47
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:11,200
to a certain set of kernel level threads okay 
and the mapping can be changing dynamically  

48
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:18,320
depending on basically the execution what your 
code is trying to do and so on

